#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184234 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7797323b-5c5c-55db-b2d9-4184556dc18a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Stretch Only Inside Parentheses"}] :question/id #uuid "2ea5d035-862f-514f-94bb-9b703d97a04f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10016" :question/problem "Given the graph of $y=f(x)$, we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n1. Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 12$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n2. Translate it down by $4$ units\n3. Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac13$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1. Stretching the graph of $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 12$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $2x$. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y = f\\left(2x \\right)$.\n2. Translating the graph of $y=f\\left(2x\\right)$ down by $4$ units is equivalent to subtracting $4$ from $f\\left(2x \\right)$. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y =f\\left(2x \\right)-4$.\n3. Stretching the graph of $y=f\\left(2x \\right)-4$ by a scale factor of $\\dfrac13$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $\\dfrac13$. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y=\\dfrac13(f\\left(2x\\right)-4) = \\dfrac13f(2x)-\\dfrac43$.\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac13f(2x)-\\dfrac43\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183917 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2252e35-f1c6-5d83-853a-9090c253fd35" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Several Transformations: Horizontal Shift Followed By Horizontal Stretch Inside Parentheses"}] :question/id #uuid "5cd41918-0c78-503d-a741-e6e7f15a3a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-10017" :question/problem "Given the graph of $y=f(x)$, we apply the following transformations in the order given:\n\n1. Stretch it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n2. Translate it to the right by $2$ units\n3. Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 15$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhat is the final function expression after applying all the transformations?" :question/worked-solution "To find the expression, let's apply each of the transformations in turn.\n\n1. Stretching the graph of $y=f\\left(x \\right)$ by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis is equivalent to multiplying the function expression by $3$. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y=3f\\left(x \\right)$.\n2. Translating the graph of $y=3f(x)$ by $2$ units to the right is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $(x-2)$. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y =3f(x-2)$.\n3. Stretching the graph of $y=3f\\left(x-2 \\right)$ by a scale factor of $\\dfrac 15$ parallel to the $x$-axis is equivalent to replacing every instance of $x$ with $5x$. Applying this transformation, we obtain $y = 3f\\left((5x)-2 \\right) =3f\\left(5x-2 \\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the final function expression is\n\n$$\ny=3f\\left(5x-2\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192405 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "421b9396-2a7b-5c45-897a-9de5f52683de" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0042ce26-8793-5690-b4ab-9f018afe979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10459" :question/problem "The function $f(x) = \\dfrac{4}{(x-1)^2}$ is defined for $x >1$. Find $f^{-1}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{4}{(y-1)^2}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (4)/((y - 1)^{2}) \\\\\nx \\cdot (y - 1)^{2} &= 4 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} &= \\frac{4}{x} \\\\\n\\sqrt{(y - 1)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{x}} \\\\\n\\mid y - 1 \\mid &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that for $y > 1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid y-1 \\mid = y-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} + 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192406 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7266d5c7-67b9-59db-b994-07dcb143af1f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Simple Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9b149c51-015f-5346-9fca-8da406d0a79a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10460" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}$." :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{2-y}{y+3}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{2 - y}{y + 3} \\\\\nx(y + 3) &= 2 - y \\\\\nxy + 3x &= 2 - y \\\\\nxy + y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny(x + 1) &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{x+1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192816 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcedd9d0-f87c-58b2-9ef1-c9fa0ef2e5c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f6ab6606-77ce-583a-9712-175825864612" :question/math-academy-id "e-10461" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$, defined as\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{kx + 6}{x-4}, \\qquad x\\neq 4$\nhas an inverse function given by\n$f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{4x+6}{x-2}, \\qquad x\\neq 2$.\nWhat is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "To find $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{kx + 6}{x-4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{ky+6}{y-4}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{ky + 6}{y - 4} \\\\\nx(y - 4) &= ky + 6 \\\\\nxy - 4x &= ky + 6 \\\\\nxy - ky &= 4x + 6 \\\\\ny(x - k) &= 4x + 6 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4x + 6}{x - k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{4x+6}{x-k}\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "961a5c28-9e1e-5b0c-bd04-63068bebd8b4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/id #uuid "2f02b5d4-640f-5212-af28-66c572d47b37" :question/math-academy-id "e-10828" :question/problem "Express $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 288^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $288^\\circ$, which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/4c/4c60c3aecabe8f625d09843ae5bdbc1e7e5525c1f0e6ab894387320c5420cab8.png)\n\nTo express\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\sin\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= 2π - θ \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{8π}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10π}{5} - \\frac{8π}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\n, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{8\\pi}{5}\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7d5897-3360-59fb-9f64-2f9f641290be" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "0a1428c6-96e8-5c82-82b2-ed51375454ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-10829" :question/problem "Express $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 252^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $252^\\circ$, which lies in the $3\\textrm{rd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/56/564054c68a924449a5730ee69f028ffe5e1b202ad8cb6ad4738b254873046ee5.png)\n\nTo express\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\cos\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= θ - π \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{5} - π \\\\\n&= \\frac{2π}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\n, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta_R = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{5}\\right) = -\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e677d9a-0be2-548e-ac29-b35be42f42ac" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "91e32306-d234-5db7-acd3-3b6d5bd8f437" :question/math-academy-id "e-10830" :question/problem "Express $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\theta_R$." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi} {3}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 240^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi} {3}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $240^\\circ$, which lies in the $3\\textrm{rd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/2a/2a6cc3cdec7887e13b502c49e3a0d5af98dda860789a5ffaef6105fdb5e477c4.png)\n\nTo express\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\tan\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= θ - π \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3} - π \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nand therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta_R = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be *positive* because the tangent ratio is *always* positive in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1519905-a736-54a1-8778-cc03d019a084" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio in Terms of a Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "5eb9b588-a263-5a0f-a3a2-96efe4bd8d08" :question/math-academy-id "e-10831" :question/problem "Express $\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\\right) = 117^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $117^\\circ$, which lies in the $2\\textrm{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/5f/5f059e8447f65aa1b321eb24665e36dd7a3a4a1628ccfdd73412acefa9ce4ee3.png)\n\nTo express\n\n$$\n\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)\n$$\n\nin terms of $\\cot\\theta_R$, we follow three steps:\n\n1. Find its reference angle $\\theta_R$.\n2. Calculate the value of the function for $\\theta_R$.\n3. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\n$$\n\nis in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= π - θ \\\\\n&= π - \\frac{13π}{20} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7π}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is\n\n$$\n\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)\n$$\n\n, and therefore we're interested in\n\n$$\n\\cot\\theta_R = \\cot\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{20}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio\n\n$$\n\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right)\n$$\n\nmust be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the 2nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{13\\pi}{20}\\right) = -\\cot\\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{20}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "bca42c78-0735-587e-914b-7c9892f47d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-10986" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0fc4d1e69587fe42eeae88a5382638fa9cf6c7c1f83c6c82a607401ab0de4cec.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(-1)$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function $f^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x)$, as depicted below.\n\n![](images/47/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(4)) &= f^{-1}(-1) \\\\\n4 &= f^{-1}(-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-1) = 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"}] :question/id #uuid "841640c2-1f50-5a13-b12b-bfe202e7621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10987" :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)$?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we have\n\n$$\nf(-4) = 3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(-4)) &= f^{-1}(3) \\\\\n- 4 &= f^{-1}(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(3) = -4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "200ba5c0-9ec6-56e9-8ce4-24cee169ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10988" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C$, and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)$?" :question/worked-solution "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{-1}(f(2)) &= f^{-1}(-3) \\\\\n2 &= f^{-1}(-3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(-3) = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "062748b1-c85a-5c2c-8d1f-f1006e73a880" :question/math-academy-id "e-10989" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{4}-2$ and $g(x)=4x-2k$. If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f$, then we must have\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x))$, we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= 4x - 2k \\\\\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}f(x)} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{x}{4}-2}\n$$\n\ninto the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(f(x)) &= 4f(x) - 2k \\\\\n&= 4(\\frac{x}{4} - 2) - 2k \\\\\n&= x - 8 - 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is the inverse of $f$, we also know that\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x\n$$\n\nEquating the above expression with $x$ and solving for $k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\nx - 8 - 2k &= x \\\\\n- 8 - 2k &= 0 \\\\\n2k &= -8 \\\\\nk &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "da62e401-d03d-54fd-90b5-410d36bdefad" :question/math-academy-id "e-1226" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}-3.$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}{:}$\n\n![](images/bf/bfe6ffc2b355b488899d29a827f779c60c13ca499312e0425c5b10b07057ed0b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}-3,$ we take the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}$ and shift it $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-2),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/2c/2c002392e3cb165c295146c3e725ba5389fcf8c285f1c6ccaf7ae457b0f0ed46.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "be6b6b07-c139-5ba2-b463-420a2ead54c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-1227" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x + 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/71/719e665784fe7ab17a0446e1ae1dae0d3acbcee30b8d4c5437003f9b93f954ce.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/ac/acf747ec793e13f14b10232c90d9a7dde80cfd414c278bbf5034a903e74b5f05.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x + 1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,3),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/c3/c342bc5ec82b31bc9d6ae49d0ecc876f2796a29b7e6df2ee833d3c8723e713c9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "5ab90279-394b-5df0-8bf3-a1042196b40c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1228" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y = k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c.$ Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf9fe67940a4257bad36511847e5cdb91974993e3be6d113dbc62dadd782134.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5$, hence $c=-5.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-1),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the function and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-5 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}-5 & =-1 \\\\ k-5 & =-1 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $y = 4\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} - 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064337 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f2e684-6cd8-5ede-9f70-1a24ada54cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "7f0eeea0-e493-5287-9633-8128758d5d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-125" :question/problem "Find the instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)=3x^2$ at the point $x=5$ using the definition." :question/worked-solution "Let's use the definition\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nIn our problem, we have $a=5$, and so by the definition we have\n\n$$\nf^{′}(5) = lim_(h → 0)(f(5 + h) - f(5))/(h)\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=3x^2\n$$\n\n, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(5) &= lim_(h → 0)((3(5 + h)^{2} - 3(5)^{2})/(h)) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{75 + 30h + 3h^{2} - 75}{h}) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(\\frac{30h + 3h^{2}}{h}) \\\\\n&= lim_(h → 0)(30 + 3h) \\\\\n&= 30 + 3(0) \\\\\n&= 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064338 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ce0d18d-f1ee-5654-984d-f191abc9a410" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point Using the Equivalent Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "95edcdc3-a675-5963-9e1b-e9eb0595327d" :question/math-academy-id "e-126" :question/problem "Using the limit of a difference quotient, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=2x-x^2$ at the point $x=2$." :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is equal to $f'(2)$.\n\nLet's use the equivalent definition for $f'(a)$, given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nUsing the above definition with $a=2$, we have\n\n$$\nf'(2) = \\lim_{x\\to 2}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(2)} {x-2}\n$$\n\nApplying this to\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-x^2\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(2) &= lim_(x → 2)(([2x - x^{2}] - [2(2) - (2)^{2}])/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(\\frac{-x^{2} + 2x}{x - 2}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)((-x(x - 2))/(x - 2)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → 2)(-x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line to the curve at $x=2$ is $-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bba19b5-e069-5b25-a7e1-00e8058afcd8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Multiplicity of a Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "d7dee7aa-bbb9-50ba-9b0c-6aa0fecbe49d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12994" :question/problem "Which root of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+1)^2(x^2 -x -2)(x-2)(x+3)$ has multiplicity $3$?" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's completely factor $P(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= (x + 1)^{2}(x^{2} - x - 2)(x - 2)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)^{2}((x + 1)(x - 2))(x - 2)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)^{2}(x + 1)(x - 2)(x - 2)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)^{3}(x - 2)^{2}(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the factored form, we can tell that the roots of $P(x)$ are $x=-1$, $x=2$, and $x=-3$.\n\n- The factor $(x+1)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $P(x)$. So the root $x=-1$ has multiplicity $3$.\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $P(x)$. So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $2$.\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $P(x)$. So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1$ (and it is a simple root).\n\nTherefore, the root with multiplicity $3$ is $x=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/id #uuid "f83ec339-8cf8-5786-a17e-063842ef9a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12995" :question/problem "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=-3$ in the polynomial $p(x) = 2x^3 + 13x^2 + 24x + 9$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x)$, the factor theorem tells us that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$.\n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$, we know that $(x+3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x)$, we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-3$ | $2$ | $13$ | $24$ | $9$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-6$ | $-21$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p(x)}{x+3} = 2x^2 + 7x + 3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x) = (x+3) (2x^2 + 7x + 3)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= (x + 3)(2x + 1)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2}(2x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/id #uuid "4600595a-b472-56e5-bce1-e57102880761" :question/math-academy-id "e-12996" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x - 12$ have multiplicity $1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= x^{3} + 3x^{2} - 4x - 12 \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x + 3) - 4(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 4)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x - 2)(x + 2)(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $1$.\n- The factor $(x+2)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=-2$ has multiplicity $1$.\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1$.\n\nIn conclusion, the roots $x=2,-2,-3$ all have multiplicity $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc0ce2c2-36b9-526b-93d6-31bf223ef326" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF"}] :question/id #uuid "c9da6c44-61e0-5b73-b1b4-fa9e4f0ac440" :question/math-academy-id "e-12997" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-5x^2$ have multiplicity $1$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial.\n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $x^3$ and $-5x^2$ is $x^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= x^{3} - 5x^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x^2$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2$.\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1$.\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198141 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "874dfb35-398e-5ed7-8a76-f0011569e6a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Complement Using a Venn Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "225ed6ce-5b8f-5570-85fe-f093c8c7632c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13072" :question/problem "![](images/95/95c65250736fdfe5989873c31623af8b493ead007a8a799f535111533e899d99.png)\n\nThe Venn diagram shown above represents two events $A$ and $B$ and the number of outcomes in each region of the sample space.\n\nGiven that every outcome is equally likely, find $P\\left(A'\\cap B\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $\\mathcal S$.\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A'\\cap B$ is represented by the area inside the circle $B$ without the intersection between the circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/b5/b576c638a88befbc04daf402922f6420fe672fbcc8ca4953a6e391b5b7fa2bb8.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A'\\cap B$ is $7$.\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $\\mathcal S$ is\n$9+3+7+11=30$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP\\left(A'\\cap B\\right)=\\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cd170a0-47b2-5747-b7a1-13521b841e37" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Given a Description of a Sample Space"}] :question/id #uuid "c613ab13-9671-528c-9e10-1f05ea0b0878" :question/math-academy-id "e-13073" :question/problem "At an audition for the school band, there are $35$ volunteer students in total. Of the volunteers, $20$ students volunteer to play percussion, $15$ students volunteer to play a wind instrument, and $3$ students volunteer for both percussion and wind instruments.\n\nRepresent the corresponding sample space using a Venn diagram. If we randomly select a student among the $35$ students, what is the probability that they volunteered for neither percussion nor wind instruments? Give your answers to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let us call $A$ the event \"The student volunteers to play percussion\" and $B$ the event \"The student volunteers to play a wind instrument.\"\n\nNote that there are $3$ students who volunteer for both percussion and wind instruments. We draw two overlapping circles representing the events $A$ and $B$ and write the number $3$ where the circles overlap.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc9e4a121d5c3fdd5afc204fcf973ec0c883a53a220846a30b267e0c5e827c1.png)\n\n- The number of students who volunteer for percussion *only* is\n$20 - 3 = 17$,\nso we write the number $17$ in the part of circle $A$ that does not overlap with circle $B$.\n\n![](images/61/61cfe75b341ac014be0f81eb4e58ac605f7680c2f9fc0e08b443e6bd2c9cecb5.png)\n\n- The number of students who volunteer for wind instruments *only* is\n$15 - 3 = 12$,\nso we write the number $12$ in the part of the circle $B$ that does not overlap with circle $A$.\n\n![](images/b7/b704fa8f8e5f77e6188ccb8bbadbb2f3bf6ed8034897b8b1d205574da35030a5.png)\n\nThe total number of students who volunteered for percussion or wind instruments is\n\n$$\n3 + 17 + 12 = 32\n$$\n\nand there are $35$ students in total, so the number of remaining students is\n\n$$\n35 - 32 = 3\n$$\n\nTo represent these $3$ students who did not volunteer for wind instruments or percussion, we write the number $3$ outside of the circles $A$ and $B$.\n\nTherefore, our final Venn diagram looks as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b87d550b2adf6399f31a3c2e8ded4496a02058e904c8682c9c50d26f6ec6d50.png)\n\nThe shaded part of the Venn diagram corresponds to students who volunteered neither for percussion nor wind instruments. The probability that a randomly selected student is from this group is given by\n\n$$\nP(A'\\cap B')=\\dfrac{3}{35} \\approx 0.09\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198144 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ace27779-e2c4-5bbf-87bc-7e5227a6e5f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions on Venn Diagrams Corresponding to Complements"}] :question/id #uuid "8df05d2a-b78f-5515-90e6-27c0264042db" :question/math-academy-id "e-13076" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be events. Sketch a Venn diagram that shows a shaded region representing the event $A' \\cup B'$." :question/worked-solution "Let's first consider the shaded region representing $A'$ and $B'$ separately.\n\n- The event $A'$ is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b8d298adbec56cf806ddac183e1865bc2908d0fbf75b1935030d491d839850.png)\n\n- The event $B'$ is represented by the shaded region below.\n\n![](images/c7/c7b8767c53739f3f44cc99eee7d1cc24621ab346247c083e9624f8a10fc512e2.png)\n\nFinally, $A' \\cup B'$ is the union of the two shaded regions shown above:\n\n![](images/6c/6cd7efdce23e23882ba6ece09c6f41c67c3678846608491a7ed83bc17c103bf2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091946 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092327 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eab94cb-5a5a-5915-ab95-963cb4c63817" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closure Properties of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "5ae8b304-5ce3-5520-8893-92a02784155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1316" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions are polynomials?\n\nI. $(5x^2-10x^3) \\div (5x)$\n\nII. $(1-x^3) \\div (x^3)$\n\nIII. $(2x^5) \\cdot (-x^3-7)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that polynomials have the following closure properties:\n\n- Polynomials are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication. This means that the sum, difference, and product of two polynomials is *always* a polynomial.\n\n- Polynomials are *not* closed under division. This means that the quotient of two polynomials is *not* always a polynomial.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our expressions in turn.\n\n- Expression I is a polynomial. Even though polynomials are *not* closed under division, the quotient of these two polynomials results in a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3})÷(5x) & =\\frac{5{x}^{2}-10{x}^{3}}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x(x-2{x}^{2})}{5x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{5x}(x-2{x}^{2})}{\\cancel{5x}} \\\\  & =x-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Expression II is *not* a polynomial. Carrying out the division, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a polynomial.\n\n- Expression III is a polynomial. It's a product of two polynomials, and polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092328 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36318568-6dfe-5ba9-b607-a2d269d05425" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Constraints on the Degree of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "53557831-390f-5b8b-b5c3-6634d425c04d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1317" :question/problem "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $2$, respectively, what is the degree of $P - Q?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $3,$ the leading term contains an $x^3.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nIn $P-Q,$ the highest-exponent term still contains an $x^3.$ There is no $x^3$ term in $Q$ that could possibly cancel out the $x^3$ term in $P.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P-Q$ must be $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a0cc5ea-7f70-5c5f-841e-01a6813da8c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Operations on Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "621d4d9b-ebfb-56ef-9614-807077aea4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1318" :question/problem "Given that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P \\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $10$,\n\nII. $P + Q$ is always a polynomial of degree $5$,\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{P}{Q}$ is sometimes a polynomial." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The highest-degree terms in $P$ and $Q$ must both contain $x^5.$ Therefore, the highest-degree term in $P \\cdot Q$ must contain $x^5 \\cdot x^5 = x^{5+5} = x^{10},$ meaning that $P \\cdot Q$ has degree $10.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=-x^5.$ Then $P+Q = x^5+(-x^5)=0,$ which is not a polynomial of degree $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. For example, suppose that $P=x^5$ and $Q=x^5.$ Then $\\dfrac{P}{Q} = \\dfrac{x^5}{x^5} = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32dd6880-3725-564a-9e85-0d39b6960c72" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-134" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ when $f(x)=x^3$." :question/worked-solution "The power rule states that if\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3\n$$\n\nwe have $n=3$. So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3 \\cdot x^{3 - 1} = 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2ff1808-d239-5625-93b7-21a98ada3fab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Negative Power Function Using the Power Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58" :question/math-academy-id "e-135" :question/problem "Calculate the derivative of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we can rewrite the function as\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{1}{x^2} = x^{-2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (-2) \\cdot x^{-2 - 1} \\\\\n&=-2x^{-3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054812 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab" :question/math-academy-id "e-136" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ when $y=\\sqrt{x}$." :question/worked-solution "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{1/2 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "654f8868-880b-53dc-ba4c-677aecb10b95" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Constant Function Using the Power Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "27baaab1-2088-5277-be24-5dfba2bfa5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-137" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 12$." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, $f'(x)=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198216 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198350 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "772da46a-8b9c-54e7-8661-a5865b1df4e3" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "b618dc4c-996c-501c-972c-1bcfdeee32be" :question/math-academy-id "e-13998" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n${3a + 2b =-2; 4a - 3b = 3$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $a$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $a$, multiply the first equation by $44$\nthe second by $44$ and add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $a$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{3}}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}{4}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ and the second equation by $-{\\color{red}{3}}$, then the $a$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $12$ and $-12$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(3a + 2b =-2) \\cdot (4); (4a - 3b = 3) \\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $a$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12a + 8b &= -8 \\\\\n- 12a + 9b &= -9 \\\\\n(8b + 9b) &= (-8 - 9) \\\\\n17b &= -17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $a$, multiply the first equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}$, the second by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-3}$, and add the equations.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198217 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198351 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cf591e2-45d5-580c-9d46-c6a8645c0513" :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "68da240e-2c79-50d7-86dd-d203d01dcb32" :question/math-academy-id "e-14001" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n${4p + 5q = 7; 3p - 2q =-12$\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $q$ from the system:\n\nTo eliminate $q$, multiply the first equation by $44$\nthe second by $44$ and add the equations." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $q$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}{5}}$, while in the second equation it is $-{\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}{2}}$ and the second equation by ${\\color{red}{5}}$, then the $q$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $10$ and $-10$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(4p + 5q = 7) \\cdot (2); (3p - 2q =-12) \\cdot (5)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $q$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8p + 10q &= 14 \\\\\n15p - 10q &= -60 \\\\\n(8p + 15p) &= (14 - 60) \\\\\n23p &= -46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $q$, multiply the first equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$, the second by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}$, and add the equations.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198218 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829fbee-ca0a-5cf7-b232-c2c6869cb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the First Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "c04ac5ff-446a-52d8-b464-88d38efd6088" :question/math-academy-id "e-14003" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the following system of equations by eliminating the variable $x{:}$\n\n${2x - 3y =-7; - 3x + 4y = 8$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the $x$-coefficient in the first equation is ${\\color{red}2}$, while in the second equation it is $-{\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}3}$ and the second equation by ${\\color{red}2}$, then the $x$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $6$ and $-6$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(2x - 3y =-7) \\cdot (3); (-3x + 4y = 8) \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $x$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 9y &= -21 \\\\\n- 6x + 8y &= 16 \\\\\n(8y - 9y) &= (16 - 21) \\\\\n- y &= -5 \\\\\ny &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=5$. However, we still need to find $x$. To do this, we substitute $y=5$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3y &= -7 \\\\\n2x - 3(5) &= -7 \\\\\n2x - 15 &= -7 \\\\\n2x &= 8 \\\\\nx &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=4$ and $y=5$, which can be written as $(4,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198219 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198353 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fc5a393-5fcd-59c9-993b-d900b790e141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations by Eliminating the Second Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "10d6a803-5114-5995-87c3-1b0e0e87a079" :question/math-academy-id "e-14004" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to the system below by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\n${4x + 3y = 3; 5x + 4y = 5$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is ${\\color{red}3}$, while in the second equation it is ${\\color{blue}4}$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by ${\\color{blue}4}$ and the second equation by $-{\\color{red}3}$, then the $y$-coefficient in the first and second equations will become $-12$ and $12$, and these will cancel when we add the equations.\n\n$$\n{(4x + 3y = 3) \\cdot (4); (5x + 4y = 5) \\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16x + 12y &= 12 \\\\\n- 15x - 12y &= -15 \\\\\n(16x - 15x) &= (12 - 15) \\\\\nx &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=-3$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x=-3$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n4(-3) + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n- 12 + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n3y &= 15 \\\\\ny &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-3$ and $y=5$, which can be written as $(-3,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073483 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073713 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92c49ea0-7ec5-5ffc-8a36-c4f46f78fda7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment"}] :question/id #uuid "68fc6ab1-0c7b-50b2-b541-137be5f5572e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14356" :question/problem "Find the midpoint of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0f0569c1cdeb9f6631a9e26d63b83f25b283892c977c8595c57e2fbb017955c4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $(x_m,y_m)$ be the midpoint between the given points $x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $x_{2}, y_{2})$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{m}, y_{m}) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-7 + 5}{2}, (3 + (-3))/(2)) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{-2}{2}, \\frac{0}{2}) \\\\\n&= (-1, 0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073484 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073714 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "ca03fd37-a09e-5546-95a5-12f0cd1657c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-14358" :question/problem "Find the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(5a,0)$ and $(-a,8b)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $(x_m,y_m)$ be the midpoint between the given points $x_{1}, y_{1})$ and $x_{2}, y_{2})$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{m}, y_{m}) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2}) \\\\\n&= ((5a + (-a))/(2), \\frac{0 + 8b}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{4a}{2}, \\frac{8b}{2}) \\\\\n&= (2a, 4b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of an Endpoint Given the Midpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "04e5232d-7f2a-5dd7-b08e-666c5cf1aba5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14360" :question/problem "Find $x+y$ if the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(-1,3)$ and $(x,y)$ is $(-2,7)$.\n\n![](images/4f/4f81075174e43d48e1a4312dfb4dcfa0f69daca5e347f7e8aedc56bd458ad4b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The given points are $x_{1}, y_{1})$ $x_{2}, y_{2})$ and $x_{m}, y_{m})$\n\nAccording to the midpoint formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{m}, y_{m}) &= (\\frac{x_{1} + x_{2}}{2}, \\frac{y_{1} + y_{2}}{2}) \\\\\n(-2, 7) &= (\\frac{-1 + x}{2}, \\frac{3 + y}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding $x$- and $y$-coordinates in the equation above gives us\n\n$$\n{-2 = \\frac{-1 + x}{2}; 7 = \\frac{3 + y}{2}⇒{x =-3; y = 11\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second endpoint is\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(-3,11)\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx+y=8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054479 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Sequence Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "c861e757-63e3-5ad3-baf2-83603cc930bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-14372" :question/problem "What is the fifth term in the Fibonacci sequence of numbers, defined by\n$a_{n+2}=a_{n+1} + a_n, \\quad a_1 = a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The fifth term of the sequence corresponds to $a_5$.\n\nTo compute $a_5$, we first need to compute $a_3$. So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n + 2} &= a_{n + 1} + a_{n} \\\\\na_{1 + 2} &= a_{1 + 1} + a_{1} \\\\\na_{3} &= a_{2} + a_{1} \\\\\na_{3} &= 1 + 1 \\\\\na_{3} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $a_4$, we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2 + 2} &= a_{2 + 1} + a_{2} \\\\\na_{4} &= a_{3} + a_{2} \\\\\na_{4} &= 2 + 1 \\\\\na_{4} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe continue this process to compute $a_5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{5} &= a_{4} + a_{3} = 3 + 2 = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fifth term of the sequence is $a_5 = 5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054480 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "549d3c1f-305c-5d21-a3cf-863fb0ff1614" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Function Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "50fbf2eb-7e2d-51f6-ac24-378afac328c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14376" :question/problem "What is the fourth term of the sequence\n\n$f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n) \\,, \\quad f(1)=-1\\,, \\quad f(2) = 3, \\quad n \\geq 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The fourth term is represented by $f(4)$.\n\nTo compute $f(4)$, we first need to compute $f(3)$. So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & = f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & = 3+(-1) \\\\ f(3)&= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4)$, we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & = f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & = 2+3 \\\\ f(4)&= 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is\n\n$$\nf(4)=5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078187 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078468 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d905f41-5940-547e-b3d2-558c6b881b68" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term in an Arithmetic Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "eec455b0-98ff-59a5-b552-3381fb893fd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-14392" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $42$ in the following arithmetic sequence?\n$-10, \\: -6, \\: -2, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a_1=-10$ and\n\n$$\nd=-6-(-10)=4\n$$\n\nwe can construct the formula for the $n$th term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-10 + (n - 1)(4) \\\\\n&=-10 + 4n - 4 \\\\\n&= 4n - 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=42$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 4n - 14 \\\\\n42 &= 4n - 14 \\\\\n56 &= 4n \\\\\n14 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $42$ is the $14$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078188 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c61d587-2441-5e8c-8da1-5bd62ff98e1e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "ff198a0e-d34e-5706-ad74-dbfa20fc3a94" :question/math-academy-id "e-14395" :question/problem "Given the arithmetic sequence\n\n$a_{n+1} = a_n + 12, \\quad a_1 = 3,\\quad n\\geq 1$,\nwhat is the index of the term $255$?" :question/worked-solution "We will convert the recursive formula to an explicit formula and then solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255$.\n\nThe recursive rule for an arithmetic sequence takes the form\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = a_n +{\\color{black}d}\n$$\n\nIn the given formula, we have\n\n$$\nd={\\color{black}12}\n$$\n\nWe are also told that\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{black}3}\n$$\n\nso we can write the explicit formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 3 + (n - 1)(12) \\\\\n&= 3 + 12n - 12 \\\\\n&= 12n - 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ such that $a_n=255$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 12n - 9 \\\\\n255 &= 12n - 9 \\\\\n264 &= 12n \\\\\n\\frac{264}{12} &= n \\\\\n22 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $255$ is the $22$nd term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078189 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bbbc551-e3ff-5cce-a572-e5b89067005a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given Another Term and the Common Difference"}] :question/id #uuid "84266116-5a1b-528a-929a-40b5d863e5a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14397" :question/problem "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_4=5$ and common difference $d=3$, what is the index of the term $95$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $a_4=5$ is $3$ jumps after $a_1$, we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1} + 3d \\\\\n5 &= a_{1} + 3(3) \\\\\n5 &= a_{1} + 9 \\\\\n- 4 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&=-4 + (n - 1)(3) \\\\\n&=-4 + 3n - 3 \\\\\n&= 3n - 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=95$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 3n - 7 \\\\\n95 &= 3n - 7 \\\\\n102 &= 3n \\\\\n\\frac{102}{3} &= n \\\\\n34 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $95$ is the $34$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078191 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d979a7bd-42a1-5d4f-8717-1caa06ce623c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Index of a Term Given Two Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "5b905b18-5ac3-5a0f-a42f-1d9e53e792cc" :question/math-academy-id "e-14399" :question/problem "Given an arithmetic sequence that has $a_2=12$ and $a_{4} = 10$, what is the index of the term $-12$?" :question/worked-solution "To construct the formula for the sequence, we need to know the common difference $d$ and the first term $a_1$.\n\nSince we are given $a_2=12$ and $a_4=10$, we can find the common difference by dividing the total difference by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{4} - a_{2}}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 - 12}{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $a_2=12$ is $1$ jump after $a_1$, we can determine the first term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2} &= a_{1} + d \\\\\n12 &= a_{1} - 1 \\\\\n13 &= a_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\n&= 13 + (n - 1)(-1) \\\\\n&= 14 - n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=-12$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 14 - n \\\\\n- 12 &= 14 - n \\\\\n- 26 &= -n \\\\\n26 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-12$ is the $26$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091951 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "2f5afc63-6988-5676-b170-5826e659a5fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-144" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y = 2x^3 - 2x$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x) = 2x^3 - 2x.$ Computing the derivative $f'(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 6x^2-2.\n$$\n\nWe then compute $f'(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\nf'(1)  = 6(1)^2-2 = 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=4$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,0),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =4(x-1) \\\\ y & =4x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091952 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c8ba29-95c9-5494-be0b-9b67bee6e45c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining where a Tangent Line Intersects an Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "e248bb90-ba83-5e3f-938b-2c8be7d6eb7a" :question/math-academy-id "e-146" :question/problem "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=4\\sqrt{x}+4x$ at the point $(1,8)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4\\sqrt{x}+4x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\sqrt{x}+4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{1/2}+4x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 2(1)^{-1/2}+4 = 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =6(x-1) \\\\ y & =6x-6+8 \\\\ y & =6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6x+2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $\\left(-\\dfrac 1 3, 0\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171743 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a23a10c1-a091-55bb-98a6-7718e46d841b" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Equation of a Parabola With Leading Coefficients in Vertex Form"}] :question/id #uuid "108ee1b6-2266-5956-b06d-4f4092fdee57" :question/math-academy-id "e-1469" :question/problem "Write the equation of the parabola $y=2x^2 - 12x + 2$ in vertex form." :question/worked-solution "To express the equation of the parabola in vertex form, we need to complete the square.\n\nFirst, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2x^2 - 12x + 2 = 2(x^2 - 6x + 1)\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 6x + 1 &= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 1 \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2} - 3^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot x + 3^{2}) - 3^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} - 3^{2} + 1 \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} - 9 + 1 \\\\\n&= (x - 3)^{2} - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x^{2} - 6x + 1) &= 2((x - 3)^{2} - 8) \\\\\n&= 2(x - 3)^{2} - 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressed in vertex form, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\ny=2(x-3)^2 - 16\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068519 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "20a59278-1d09-5519-a362-4634ac9d5144" :question/math-academy-id "e-1587" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{x} + c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](images/25/257c44bbc3762968aee94b2612f489af9f2eda7eb8b5473d5d20f6f3f989cbc9.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y = \\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x$ is $y = 0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y=\\left( \\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1.$ Therefore, $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054678 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "519b6d45-2821-5bf2-b837-97d8a1ae9314" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Mean From Grouped Data"}] :question/id #uuid "e476b6a9-88bd-5897-8c0c-bd8859a5f18b" :question/math-academy-id "e-16511" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7ff4e40e3a505af29013dded0c58b2bde88bc826d612e68774034ef288309ec8.png)\n\nA gym personal trainer recorded the duration of an exercise session for a group of $10$ gym visitors. The results are presented above in the form of a histogram. Find an estimate of the mean duration of these exercise sessions." :question/worked-solution "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the mean can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline x \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^K f_i \\cdot m_{i}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f_i$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n- $m_i$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class, and\n- $n$ is the total number of observations.\n\nTo estimate the mean, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each class and the products of the midpoints and frequencies.\n\n| Duration ($\\textrm{min}$) | $m_i$ | $f_i$ | $m_i \\cdot f_i$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $40 \\leq d \\lt 50$ | $45$ | $4$ | $180$ |\n| $50 \\leq d \\lt 60$ | $55$ | $2$ | $110$ |\n| $60 \\leq d \\lt 70$ | $65$ | $3$ | $195$ |\n| $70 \\leq d \\lt 80$ | $75$ | $1$ | $75$ |\n\nFinally, we sum the values in the last column and divide by $10$ (the total number of observations).\n\n$$\nd^(―) \\mid \\approx \\frac{180 + 110 + 195 + 75}{10}; = \\frac{560}{10}; = 56min\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean duration of these exercise sessions is approximately $56\\,\\textrm{min}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054679 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0996a6f2-3c15-5670-891a-34fd6ecc8b72" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/id #uuid "96d65347-9223-549a-b214-30942fecc794" :question/math-academy-id "e-16512" :question/problem "A retailer tracks the weekly sales of a popular product for several years. The number of units sold per week is recorded in the frequency table. Estimate the standard deviation of the sales data.\n\n| Units sold ($x_i$) | Frequency $(f_i)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $10 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $30$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 30$ | $20$ |\n| $30 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $50$ |\n\nGiven that an estimate of the mean weekly sale is $\\overline x \\approx 27$ units per week, find an estimate for the standard deviation $\\sigma_n$." :question/worked-solution "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the variance can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_n \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^K f_i \\cdot (m_{i} - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f_i$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n- $m_i$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n- $\\overline x$ is the mean.\n\nTo find the variance, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each interval, the squared deviations of the midpoints from the mean, and the products of the squared deviations and frequencies.\n\n| $x_i$ | $f_i$ | $m_i$ | $\\left(m_i - \\overline x\\right)^2$ | $f_i \\left(m_i - \\overline x\\right)^2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $10 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $30$ | $15$ | $144$ | $4320$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 30$ | $20$ | $25$ | $4$ | $80$ |\n| $30\\leq x \\lt 40$ | $50$ | $35$ | $64$ | $3200$ |\n\nThen, we divide the sum of all the values in the last column by the total number of observations:\n\n$$\nσ_{n}^{2} \\mid \\approx \\frac{4320 + 80 + 3200}{30 + 20 + 50}; = \\frac{7600}{100}; = 76\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{n} &= \\sqrt{76} \\approx 8.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to one decimal place, the standard deviation is $8.7$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212754 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213003 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5cf64df7-0abb-5b42-bed6-b3664c2de89a" :knowledge-point/title "Matching a Single Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "343c42bc-2a8b-5979-9a21-accb89835444" :question/math-academy-id "e-18859" :question/problem "The following equation is true for all values of $x.$\n\n$$\nmx^2 + 5x + 4 = 2x^2 + 5x + 4\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of the constant $m?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nWe compare the quadratic terms on both sides. On the left-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $m.$ On the right-hand side, the coefficient of $x^2$ is $2.$\n\nSo, we set\n\n$$\nm = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212755 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213004 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2caeb2c-39cf-595a-8105-67d11bf0e45e" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Two Coefficients"}] :question/id #uuid "0538ef73-5254-5d99-b709-b08c9ff89c11" :question/math-academy-id "e-18860" :question/problem "The equation\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + 12 = 7x^2 + 2x + 12\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $a$ and $b$  are constants. What is the value of $a + b?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the coefficients of the matching powers of $x$ must be equal.\n\nLet's compare the coefficients:\n\n- The coefficient of $x^2$ on the left-hand side is $a,$ and on the right-hand side it is $7.$ So, we have \n\n$$\na = 7.\n$$\n\n- The coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side is $b,$ and on the right-hand side it is $2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nb = 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =7+2 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212756 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213005 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46822f28-8863-5150-a420-09c61a5c28b1" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Factored Forms"}] :question/id #uuid "802b17c9-3c7d-58b9-b7ee-8632f4f1cd13" :question/math-academy-id "e-18861" :question/problem "The equation\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = (x + 3)(x + k)\n$$\n\nis true for all values of $x,$ where $k$ is a constant. What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the equation is true for all values of $x,$ the expressions on both sides must be exactly the same. That means the factored form on the right-hand side must expand to match the left-hand side.\n\nLet's expand the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+k) & ={x}^{2}+kx+3x+3k \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(k+3)x+3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compare the expanded form to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 9x + 18 = x^2 + (k + 3)x + 3k\n$$\n\nEquating the constant terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =3k \\\\ 6 & =k \\\\ k & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that value $k = 6$ produces the correct linear coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+3 & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich matches the coefficient of $x$ on the left-hand side. So, our solution is valid."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212788 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213066 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "098734a8-c226-5bc7-b174-7c0d794e986c" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Linear Functions Using Slope-Intercept Form"}] :question/id #uuid "aaf7aedd-ad76-5b8d-bb6a-3925f8d41006" :question/math-academy-id "e-18920" :question/problem "A linear function $g(x)$ gives the remaining mobile data, in gigabytes, on a monthly plan after $x$ days of continuous usage. There are $48$ GB remaining after $2$ days, and $30$ GB remaining after $5$ days. Which equation defines $g(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "The linear function modeling this situation is\n\n$$\ng(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nWe know two points on the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x_{1}, y_{1}) &= (2, 48) \\\\\n(x_{2}, y_{2}) &= (5, 30)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the given two points, we find the slope $m{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30 - 48}{5 - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-18}{3} \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&=-6x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we substitute one of the points, say $(2, 48)$, into this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n48 &= -6 \\cdot 2 + b \\\\\n48 &= -12 + b \\\\\nb &= 60\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $g(x)$ is\n\n$$\ng(x) = -6x + 60\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212789 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213068 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "410a9971-d4c0-57ff-b936-58621a2313b5" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Functions With Alternative Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "4fb71375-cea2-5364-b809-c844c462a1a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-18921" :question/problem "A theme park charges $\\[math]65$ per person for the first $30$ people in a group and $\\[/math]50$ for each additional person. Which function $C$ gives the total cost, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where $g \\geq 30$?" :question/worked-solution "The total cost consists of two parts.\n\n- For the first $30$ people, the park charges\n\n$$\n65 \\times 30 = 1,950\\,\\,\\textrm{dollars}\n$$\n\n- For the additional persons beyond $30$, the park charges\n\n$$\n50(g - 30)\\,\\,\\textrm{dollars}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost $C$, in dollars, for a group of $g$ people, where\n\n$$\ng\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC(g) &= 1, 950 + 50(g - 30) \\\\\n&= 1, 950 + 50g - 1, 500 \\\\\n&= 50g + 450\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212793 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213067 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3da0835-3969-564f-b987-a019aca8fb55" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Functions From Tabular Data"}] :question/id #uuid "604f7244-01c2-597e-9590-b7ab3965d816" :question/math-academy-id "e-18926" :question/problem "| $x$ | $r(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $6$ | $22$ |\n| $9$ | $31$ |\n| $12$ | $40$ |\n\nSome values of the linear function $r$ are shown in the table above. Which of the following defines $r(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r$ is a linear function, we know it must be of the form\n\n$$\nr(x) = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $m$ is the slope, and\n- $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\nFirst, we find the slope using two points. For $x = 6$ and $x = 9$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (r(x_{2}) - r(x_{1}))/(x_{2} - x_{1}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{31 - 22}{9 - 6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(x) &= mx + b \\\\\n&= 3x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the $y$-intercept using one point. For $x = 6$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(6) &= 3(6) + b \\\\\n22 &= 18 + b \\\\\nb &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the linear equation that defines $r(x)$ is\n\n$$\nr(x) = 3x + 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9139c75-1189-5b78-9d82-023f5208a490" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "659e7fd3-3502-5a47-a54c-3d0c33150d73" :question/math-academy-id "e-18928" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{1}{3-\\sqrt a}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $3+\\sqrt a$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $3+\\sqrt a$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3 - \\sqrt{a}} \\cdot \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{3 + \\sqrt{a}} &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/((3 - \\sqrt{a})(3 + \\sqrt{a})) &=  \\\\\n(3 + \\sqrt{a})/(3^{2} - (\\sqrt{a})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{a}}{9 - a}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa5d982f-ff13-5a31-9cd3-d5847ea2790c" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing a Binomial Within the Radical"}] :question/id #uuid "dfb7701e-d11b-52a0-97fb-8363b1ff0901" :question/math-academy-id "e-18929" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+1}-1$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+1}-1$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x} {\\sqrt{x+1}+1}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+1}-1}{\\sqrt{x+1}-1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1}+1)(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {(\\sqrt{x+1})^2-1^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x+1-1} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {x(\\sqrt{x+1}-1)} {x} \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{x+1}-1 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186212796 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186213019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "43395cdf-3e16-5147-87fb-371193078b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing a Denominator With Two Radicals"}] :question/id #uuid "4bcf9ec5-b5a1-5ab2-bb9f-f22280e072ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-18930" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}}$." :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}$.\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{x})(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{(\\sqrt{2})^2-(\\sqrt{x})^2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{x(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{x})}{2-x} \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147226 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147462 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "631bc93f-4596-526f-9652-f35137b60883" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Using a Formula Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9d8383ad-c617-5b01-8055-214a8c639f12" :question/math-academy-id "e-2175" :question/problem "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $2$ and whose seventh term is $-686$." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_7 = -686$ and $a_4 = 2$. Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{7 - 4} &= \\frac{a_{7}}{a_{4}} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{-686}{2} \\\\\nr^{3} &= -343\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{3} &= -343 \\\\\nr^{3} &= (-7)^{3} \\\\\nr &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147463 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df9158c-01a9-5596-9528-6033296b2925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Ratio Given Some Additional Information About the Geometric Sequence"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa4b1ba-4362-5a4b-88d4-25d9ebe2cef7" :question/math-academy-id "e-2178" :question/problem "The third term of a geometric sequence is $80$ and its fifth term is $20$. Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms $a_m$ and $a_n$, we use the formula\n\n$$\nr^{n-m} = \\dfrac{a_n}{a_m}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given $a_5 = 20$ and $a_3 = 80$. Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{5 - 3} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{3}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{20}{80} \\\\\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4} \\\\\nr^{2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147461 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81a78a4d-f4ef-5c51-b34e-22d3ee51c7c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Equation for the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "84364858-da8e-5241-85ca-ec84057bc6e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-2179" :question/problem "Find the equation for the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $6$ and whose $9$th term is $192$." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1r^{n-1}\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=4$ and $n=9$, respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{4} &= a_{1}r^{4 - 1} = a_{1}r^{3}, \\\\\na_{9} &= a_{1}r^{9 - 1} = a_{1}r^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $a_4 = 6$ and $a_9 = 192$, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{a_{1}r^{3} = 6; a_{1}r^{8} = 192\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{a_{1}r^{8}}{a_{1}r^{3}} &= \\frac{192}{6} \\\\\n\\frac{r^{8}}{r^{3}} &= 32 \\\\\nr^{5} &= 32\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05b40dd4-a99b-5d5e-93b4-767ed99d62a0" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Two Events by Likelihood"}] :question/id #uuid "4ced75f7-e2c9-50f2-b2cf-114222fc960d" :question/math-academy-id "e-24942" :question/problem "A scientist records how long it takes for a chemical reaction to complete. The experiment is conducted $40$ times. The table below shows the results.\n\n| Time (seconds) | Frequency |\n| --- | --- |\n| $<10$ | $6$ |\n| $10-20$ | $14$ |\n| $20-30$ | $8$ |\n| $30-40$ | $7$ |\n| $>40$ | $5$ |\n\nBased on the experimental probabilities of these results, complete the following statements.\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{unlikely}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{as likely as not}}}.$\n\n$\\qquad$A reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{\\phantom{\\text{less likely than}}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "One event is more likely than another if it has a greater probability of occurring. If two events have the same probability, they are equally likely.\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took more than $30$ seconds $(7+5=12)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/41/41ae3b0ee5b77a5967ee949abfd5dc5327fc840e9061cecf1dfd52728405415f.svg)\n\nThis probability is a small number, closer to $0.$ Since\n\n$$\n0 < \\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12,\n$$\n\nthis event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{unlikely}}.$\n\n- To compute the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds, we divide the number of times a reaction took less than $20$ seconds $(6+14=20)$ by the total number of trials $(40).$ This gives\n![](images/19/194542c8095442fe631a733e681d43b025ac5b5f7f478f7b3c541e3c51718c69.svg)\n\nSince the probability is $\\dfrac12,$ this event is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{as likely as not}}.$\n\n- The experimental probability of a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is less than the experimental probability of a reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{10} < \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a reaction completing in more than $30$ seconds is\n$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{less likely than}}$\na reaction completing in less than $20$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147290 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8fd3c2c-3340-50df-a95c-3b82f165a071" :knowledge-point/title "Naming Prisms"}] :question/id #uuid "319d252c-a8c4-5a5f-ad2b-f4bdb5702b75" :question/math-academy-id "e-2662" :question/problem "What is the name of the polyhedron shown below?\n\n![](images/b6/b6b4021c8add7d92bfbc98c23f9c9e56e4fd0c8fa152e60c04ca78c5c5b594cd.png)" :question/worked-solution "The polyhedron is a **heptagonal prism** since it has two identical heptagonal faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal) prism since it does not have an octagonal (pentagonal or hexagonal) base.\n- The solid is not a cube since not all of its faces are squares."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147291 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9d83422-a877-5de8-bc55-2c7aae9eeb72" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Polyhedra"}] :question/id #uuid "0583c82a-049c-51e6-aebc-bcd32423824e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2668" :question/problem "Which of the following is **not** a polyhedron?\n\n![](images/ce/ceedc03f96a3c801f31069f5551a8c0e5241dbabfc5c9463240a1eb1698a7105.png)" :question/worked-solution "Each face in a polyhedron must be a polygon. Therefore, the only option that is **not** a polyhedron is:\n\n![](images/ae/aea63ad8ff248347de606d37112355cdecc30ab5ae631b987b87f0b9f9d68f17.png)\n\nNotice that one of its faces is not a polygon (it has curved sides)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "159b4d58-b34d-52cd-aad2-2c058f772ad2" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b7051104-6e23-5ab2-8779-5d54a3c3f5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-270" :question/problem "Consider a spherical balloon with radius $r.$ As the balloon loses air, its volume $V$ decreases. What is the best interpretation of $V'(r)?$\n\n![](images/7f/7f52288ca4670375413ecd5283bc2d15fbb332af311ead07986c1526d25e338a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function $V(r)$ gives the volume of a sphere with radius $r.$ So\n\n$$\nV'(r) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}V}{\\textrm{d}r} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{Change in }V}{\\textrm{Change in }r}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $V'(r)$ is the rate at which the volume changes as the radius changes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2451a654-2255-5867-871d-54340dfeff81" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Derivative of a Function in a Given Context"}] :question/id #uuid "8378ead2-9247-55a0-acc8-2e6d0874ea09" :question/math-academy-id "e-271" :question/problem "The function $B(s)$ models a school's budget, in dollars, given the average number of students $s$ in each grade. What is the best interpretation of $B'(30)=2\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $B'(s)$ represents the rate at which the budget is changing at the moment when the school has an average of exactly $s$ students in each grade.\n\nTherefore, $B'(30) = 2\\,000$ means:\n\n*When the school has an average of $30$ students in each grade, its budget is increasing at a rate of $2\\,000$ dollars per student.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064037 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "750e7350-1434-56ef-80d8-7cf531108f96" :question/math-academy-id "e-273" :question/problem "On a particular day, the rate $E$ at which tourists enter a small country can be modeled as $E(t) = 50\\cdot 3^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, after midnight. The rate at which tourists leave the country can be modeled as $L(t)=100\\cdot 2^{0.2t}$ tourists per hour, where $t$ is also the time in hours after midnight.\n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}?$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of the number of tourists in the country at time $t$ is equal to the entry rate minus the exit rate at time $t,$ that is, \n\n$$\nE(t)-L(t).\n$$\n\nSince $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is ten hours after midnight, we have $t=10.$ Substituting $t=10,$ the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(10)-L(10) & =50\\cdot {3}^{0.2(10)}-100\\cdot {2}^{0.2(10)} \\\\  & =450-400 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of tourists in the country at $10\\text{:}00\\,\\textrm{am}$ is $50$ tourists per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147294 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddd0beef-ff0c-5ef3-88c4-ce49fbb8d5cb" :knowledge-point/title "Naming Pyramids"}] :question/id #uuid "dc558592-e65f-5369-890d-fffc633f2a3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-2757" :question/problem "![](images/be/beed0799de4f42cde58bf0c85f31a1a6850ef1227ea2827e9819a4edeb3ede4a.png)\n\nWhat is the name of the polyhedron shown above?\n\n1. Hexagonal prism\n2. Hexagonal pyramid\n3. Rectangular prism" :question/worked-solution "The solid is a **hexagonal pyramid** since its base is a hexagon and all other $6$ faces meet at a single point\n\nAlso, notice that:\n\n- The solid is not a hexagonal prism since it doesn't have two identical hexagonal faces connected by equal parallel lines.\n- The solid is not a rectangular prism since not all of its faces are rectangles."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100620 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100952 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a649e49-73c7-5470-8361-2002da74f234" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "04538f07-6e4c-547e-be0c-8b62025e19ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-28" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} x^{-5}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we will express the negative exponent as a quotient:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} x^{-5}= \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\dfrac{1}{x^5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)x^{-5} &= (lim_(x → 2)1)/(lim_(x → 2)x^{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2^{5}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100621 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100953 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0159149-010c-5bef-a28d-85c4c43f0586" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "71b3f83c-33e6-5043-9215-7db7b4b12497" :question/math-academy-id "e-29" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\dfrac{xf(x)+x^2}{2+f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/5b/5b29133a4e82b885aa3e20e423719564a61bfff5b33472addf5036d3a18efd3c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2})/(2 + f(x)) &= (lim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2}))/(lim_(x → 2)(2 + f(x)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2}) &= 2(3) + 2^{2} = 10, \\\\\nlim_(x → 2)(2 + f(x)) &= 2 + 3 = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2})/(2 + f(x)) &= (lim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2}))/(lim_(x → 2)(2 + f(x))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{5} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133546 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "811ba45c-7467-5cef-9fb8-a8282891b607" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Rigid Motions"}] :question/id #uuid "25f331e5-db8f-58bd-a4a6-24d7ed09db10" :question/math-academy-id "e-2906" :question/problem "The quadrilateral $ABCD$ is rotated by an angle $\\alpha$ counterclockwise about the point $A$. The resulting polygon is $A'B'C'D'$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ coincides with $A'$\n2. $\\angle ACB \\cong \\angle A'C'B'$\n3. $AB = A'B'$" :question/worked-solution "The given transformation is a rotation of $\\alpha$ counterclockwise about the point $A$, which is a rigid motion. Rigid motions preserve the distances between all points and all angle measures.\n\n![](images/73/73d6148ffe8d269f7846cf95679cddef30e058380aef6546fb8bd7577d9e9b80.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Under the action of the transformation, the point $A$ is left unchanged. Therefore, $A=A'$.\n- Statement II is true. Under the action of the transformation, the measures of all corresponding angles are preserved. Therefore, $\\angle{ACB}\\cong \\angle{A'C'B'}$.\n- Statement III is true. Under the action of the transformation, the distance between all corresponding points is preserved. Therefore, $AB = A'B'$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133977 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe2598e-57bd-53fb-8477-9c4c5a8d3fa4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Polygons That Map to Each Other Under Rigid Motions"}] :question/id #uuid "46db11d7-8448-54f5-a5ea-46abfc746574" :question/math-academy-id "e-2907" :question/problem "Which of the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$, $\\mathcal{R}$, $\\mathcal{S}$ shown below can be obtained from the polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ with rigid motions?\n\n![](images/ab/abd2df7fd01c1d0b04bb9b25a2df48738532d12cab70c1d24038a018e04dd2db.png)" :question/worked-solution "By transforming $\\mathcal{Q}$ using only rigid motions, we can get only polygons congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$.\n\nSimilarly, any polygon congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$ can be obtained from $\\mathcal{Q}$ using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{R}$ is not congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$, it can't be obtained from $\\mathcal{Q}$ using rigid motions.\n\nThe polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{S}$ are congruent to $\\mathcal{Q}$. Therefore, we can obtain them by applying a combination of rigid motions to $\\mathcal{Q}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133548 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4ffa315-2f50-51da-a928-329e1816706f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Why Polygons Are Congruent Using Rigid Motions"}] :question/id #uuid "d928641d-fd70-5cbb-a217-e9e454a8bd51" :question/math-academy-id "e-2909" :question/problem "In terms of rigid motions, why are the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ congruent?\n\n![](images/1d/1de244f4d4ad5ff86c6c300025edbc50e43415855487ae73e704478802e780e8.png)" :question/worked-solution "The polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ are congruent because $\\mathcal{Q}$ is the image of $\\mathcal{P}$ under the action of rotation by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise around the origin.\n\n![](images/66/668fe5f1f5e0092a5ae6daceeada015ce6d84b25afea80570fea1aae318edc75.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b01281fd-5272-579c-98d5-ca5cc00c73e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Maps Presented in Function Notation That Are Rigid Motions"}] :question/id #uuid "5fb05996-774b-5a89-9cc1-6876cc60338e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2912" :question/problem "Which of the following transformations map every polygon to a congruent polygon?\n\n1. $(x,y) \\mapsto (x-3, y-4)$\n2. $(x,y) \\mapsto (2x, 2y)$\n3. $(x,y) \\mapsto (y, x)$" :question/worked-solution "A rigid motion is a transformation that preserves distances. There are three types of rigid motions:\n\n- Translations\n- Rotations\n- Reflections\n\nAlso, two polygons are congruent if and only if one can be mapped to another using a combination of rigid motions.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I is a translation of $3$ units to the left and $4$ units down. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n- Transformation II is a dilation of scale factor $2$. This is *not* a rigid motion. So, it does not map every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n- Transformation III is a reflection in the line $y=x$. This is a rigid motion. So, it maps every polygon to a congruent polygon.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7545aba-d80b-58ab-9a56-25ad196da3ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Similarity Transformation Given a Polygon and Its Image"}] :question/id #uuid "54f05d2d-1137-5bc7-b56d-0215dc3895ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-2978" :question/problem "![](images/44/44a9e1bab3de94acd0a1195f1bbb8b09372e651b4938981542374c2b6fb96370.png)\n\nIf the figures $\\mathcal P$ and $\\mathcal Q$ are similar, then what transformation maps $\\mathcal P$ onto $\\mathcal Q$?" :question/worked-solution "The figures $\\mathcal P$ and $\\mathcal Q$ are similar. The ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are all equal, and all corresponding angles are equal.\n\nIndeed, $\\mathcal Q$ is the image of $\\mathcal P$ under a dilation with center at the origin and scale factor\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/47/47df13ba70a02c17afc5269d471e6381e8cc980ee0f89380f656ae625b761c9b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8b5ef8e-6f13-5496-ae7f-c9fbaf3e25c7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Similarity Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "a75b57a8-4819-594a-9aa2-e35b54a68f6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2993" :question/problem "Which of the following transformations map any polygon to a similar polygon?\n\n1. $(x,y) \\mapsto (-x, y)$\n2. $(x,y) \\mapsto (x, y-1)$\n3. $(x,y) \\mapsto (3x, 2y)$" :question/worked-solution "A similarity transformation is any transformation made up of rigid motions (rotations, reflections, and translations) and dilations.\n\nA similarity transformation will map any polygon to a similar polygon.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the transformations in turn.\n\n- Transformation I represents a reflection in the $y$-axis. Thus, it is a rigid motion and, therefore, a similarity transformation.\n- Transformation II represents a translation by $1$ unit downward. Thus, it is a similarity transformation.\n- Transformation III represents a stretch with different stretch factors in the $x$- and $y$ directions. This is not a similarity transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24d0f296-256c-5da4-8992-e2112dfe99b6" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "927b97a7-437e-5c46-b83d-1e8d2d62007e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3155" :question/problem "Construct a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $105^\\circ$ in the standard position." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/46/46e41d84cb00ea2a95f3d1c94e75863707fddf78ea7117de1ed415b71e66f2f1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6435eb94-76f8-5e46-b005-03451ab6eba2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Angles in Radians in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "02cd545d-7fde-520b-924e-5ff4f8e4244b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3156" :question/problem "Construct a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ in the standard position." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees.\n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi} 5\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = 72^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $72^\\circ$.\n\nThen, we recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, its vertex at the origin, and it rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/64/6479f08add76d919644c8eba7df6bb1d273fd1de808d4af58876a6a5da539fcb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50de296d-56a1-5166-aa7d-42de027a33bc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Trigonometry to Find an Angle in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "50e22612-2f22-5c48-a2da-a6a0580375a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3157" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0347081906740a955f8a192baa53c65536b110df9adc6021288c43767c9ac2f.png)\n\nGiven that the point $A$ has coordinates $(\\sqrt 3, 1)$, calculate the angle $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "First, we can form a triangle by creating a vertical line from $A$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/3e/3e5ebde9ee4132e0bd64cb2aaf2a3763abc2b0de1a798e87ed4ee652ca360078.png)\n\nUsing the tangent ratio, we have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 3} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the inverse tangent, we get\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{3} \\right)} = 30^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04ac62a3-1302-5011-937b-9fb20cb7fe95" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Angles in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "98f445f6-806d-5169-81f9-183b93e5bb94" :question/math-academy-id "e-3161" :question/problem "How is the angle $150^\\circ$ normally represented in the coordinate plane?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1dcf96192164d9e1845207773d4fe9596ff3fd0d5fa7e83984260004e8bc32aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aaab9fd-0ef0-5240-9142-d84b66df0eeb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Circle Given the Radius"}] :question/id #uuid "57ebd9c4-2338-5beb-8b58-0902de3d7f33" :question/math-academy-id "e-3183" :question/problem "What is the area of a circle that has a radius of $\\sqrt 2$?" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\nSubstituting the radius\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcef46b2-41a9-58df-b11d-ef58f0b065a9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Circle Given the Diameter"}] :question/id #uuid "57533c91-7a96-52c7-b9a4-fee398a961b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3184" :question/problem "What is the area of a circle that has a diameter of $24$?\n\n![](images/9c/9cf9119f565f252c632c7fe2407d9f693847c72ef3b8fc3ddf03ffbf0cb3ce1b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The radius of a circle is half the diameter. So, the radius of our circle is $r=12$.\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 12^{2} \\\\\n&= 144π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ad198fa-fde8-5d2a-baa5-a890951fb0df" :knowledge-point/title "Areas of Circles: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "41291efa-9dcd-5988-ac32-3a85f82bf5e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3186" :question/problem "The window in the attic of Mary's house has the shape of a quarter circle, as shown below. If the area of ​​the window is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\,\\textrm{m}^2$, what is the diameter of the corresponding circle?\n\n![](images/1f/1f22d702fbf4504b14787f71a45e3821cdac6d3a90acb40c1a49ce498d28f986.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the circle is four times the area of the quarter section. So,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=4\\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} =2\\pi\\,\\textrm{m}^2\n$$\n\nThe formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\n, where $r$ is the radius. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= πr^{2} \\\\\n2π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n2π &= πr^{2} \\\\\n2 &= r^{2} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{2}m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the diameter is\n\n$$\nd=2\\cdot r= 2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073616 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073672 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b7cb13a-a561-5a95-a87c-5d2f2a59b08e" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Trigonometric Ratio as a Cofunction of a Complementary Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "83830700-8d9b-5205-8f3e-764247162987" :question/math-academy-id "e-3266" :question/problem "Express $\\sin 20^\\circ$ as a cofunction of a complementary angle." :question/worked-solution "The cofunction of $\\sin x$ is $\\cos x$, and the sine of an angle is the cosine of its complement.\n\nThe angle complementary to $20^\\circ$ is\n\n$$\n90^\\circ-20^\\circ = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\sin 20^\\circ=\\cos(90^\\circ - 20^\\circ)=\\cos{70^\\circ}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f46344-b7c0-51a1-918c-aaac34dd5c40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Variable Satisfying a Cofunction Equality"}] :question/id #uuid "320908eb-adaf-5516-9810-a4fae4dc8f38" :question/math-academy-id "e-3268" :question/problem "Find a value of $x$ satisfying the equation $\\tan 35^\\circ = \\cot x$." :question/worked-solution "Since the tangent of an angle equals the cotangent of its complement, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan 35^{∘} &= \\cot (90^{∘} - 35^{∘}) = \\cot 55^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=55^\\circ$ satisfies the given equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073674 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1ec0eb3-ea8e-5783-b6a2-dc7128709f71" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Trigonometric Ratio With a Linear Expression Angle as a Cofunction of a Complementary Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "27b53e09-978d-5d78-aa3f-ad9c780adfeb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3270" :question/problem "Express $\\cos \\left(\\theta - 15^\\circ \\right)$ as a cofunction of a complementary angle." :question/worked-solution "We know that the cofunction of $\\cos\\theta$ is $\\sin\\theta$, and the angle complementary to $\\theta - 15^\\circ$ is\n\n$$\n90^\\circ-(\\theta - 15^\\circ) = 90^\\circ - \\theta + 15^\\circ = 105^\\circ - \\theta\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\theta - 15^\\circ \\right) = \\sin\\Bigl(90^\\circ-(\\theta - 15^\\circ)\\Bigr) = \\sin \\left(105^\\circ - \\theta \\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073619 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b653137f-4991-5388-b34d-8573c58c0841" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable Given Two Angles and a Cofunction Equality"}] :question/id #uuid "bd79dabb-70aa-58d8-9273-f15fed5d65cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3271" :question/problem "Given that $m\\angle A=\\left(2x+10\\right)^\\circ$ and $m\\angle B=\\left(3x+30\\right)^\\circ$, solve $\\sin A = \\cos B$ for $x$. Suppose that both $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ are acute." :question/worked-solution "If both angles are acute and\n\n$$\n\\sin A = \\cos B\n$$\n\nthen the angles $\\angle A$ and $\\angle B$ must be complementary, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} m\\angle A+m\\angle B &= 90^\\circ \\ \\left(2x+10\\right)^\\circ+ \\left(3x+30\\right)^\\circ &= 90^\\circ \\\\ (2x+10) + (3x+30) &= 90 \\\\ 5x+40 &= 90 \\\\ 5x&=50 \\\\ x &= 10. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105903 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "acc86fb6-2ef4-5f8f-b204-6e1fdbbf0530" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "62405619-31ca-5403-aa45-360e6b3fbd59" :question/math-academy-id "e-3289" :question/problem "Find the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ below. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n![](images/dc/dc625408598aa3cbfda44ec877c9a26ab563dee9fcf757192fcf2cf8411f606a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}⁡{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\,038) \\\\  & ≈597.44,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105561 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105905 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3173d361-b91d-5699-97d8-6595ef59d0a3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/id #uuid "b41b2354-c339-5398-ab43-5886b8447c1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3294" :question/problem "Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\n![](images/bc/bc9f32a9c0e8b042498a72e808a29274bd505e786d8ec43d0e0bc9888fdf6e44.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈432.022\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈177.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABC}+{A}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & ≈609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105562 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105906 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dd3dcff-06d2-5d61-9863-0f5b541d8d4b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of a Triangle Given the Area of a Quadrilateral"}] :question/id #uuid "472216c8-da7d-58fb-9056-e71d5e33460d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3297" :question/problem "The area of $ABCD$ is $300\\, \\text{cm}^2.$  What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n![](images/c0/c06ffb123bdd24c3f83419767b6d1578223b8093089b79f91aae8f1fc6121305.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & ≈376\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & ={A}_{ACD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078276 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19695af-0879-5a00-b400-1c4a490f7bfa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "82814727-3ad9-5e62-a491-086484491d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3558" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below?\n\n1. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2}f(x)=0$\n2. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n3. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)$\n\n![](images/de/dea690cf5bae7ef2463bd2f274bbc50becaa6d24f8d4aed926d9c0fda7f5e6d1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We see from the graph that $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^-}f(x)=\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2^+}f(x)=0$. Consequently, $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,2}f(x)=0$.\n- Statement II is true. When approaching $x=1$ from the left and from the right, we get\n$\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)=0, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)=3$,\nrespectively. Since $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^-}f(x)\\ne\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1^+}f(x)$, we conclude that $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$.\n- Statement III is false. Both limits $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,-1}f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\,0}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $[-1,0)$. We *cannot* say that the limits are equal because we can't compare the undefined values.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100658 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23567a86-8e1e-5b94-bac6-09c79cdb42c9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "0f441b48-b977-538c-82af-aa40ed4076d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3561" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\left(\\dfrac{x^3 f(x)}{4}\\right)$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/f4/f41914a12cf2ce0ec4799c4e9bacc3347e133f99747512a2211428e3571c4677.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we get that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=4\n$$\n\nSo, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)((x^{3}f(x))/(4)) &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(x → 2)x^{3} \\cdot lim_(x → 2)f(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2^{3} \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100659 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5feb914d-efad-5d89-85a9-db99befc9928" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit"}] :question/id #uuid "7a24a8e0-768e-541c-aff1-cad6f977da87" :question/math-academy-id "e-3562" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2}(x^2-3x)(x+1)^{3}$." :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, followed by the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 2)(x^{2} - 3x)(x + 1)^{3} &= lim_(x → - 2)(x^{2} - 3x) \\cdot lim_(x → - 2)(x + 1)^{3} \\\\\n&= (lim_(x → - 2)x^{2} - 3lim_(x → - 2)x)(lim_(x → - 2)(x^{3} + 3x^{2} + 3x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((-2)^{2} - 3(-2))((-2)^{3} + 3(-2)^{2} + 3(-2) + 1) \\\\\n&= (4 + 6)(-8 + 12 - 6 + 1) \\\\\n&= (10)(-1) \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059840 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b406f464-6326-5dbf-b35c-8cde390a65de" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine or Cosine of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "e8b9ae0a-cc32-558d-9b59-f7c7608a4eaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3863" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sin{270^\\circ}$." :question/worked-solution "The angle $270^\\circ$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $270^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1)$.\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{270^\\circ} = -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059841 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b547a211-99ec-5325-8d76-da3f7da13174" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent or Cotangent of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "33f8f1c3-eeca-5092-804d-ab6cd468d5c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3864" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point $(0,-1)$.\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$.\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1$.\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan{\\theta} = \\dfrac{\\sin \\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)}{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)} = \\dfrac{-1}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nis undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059517 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059842 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2e28247-1113-5d9f-9c76-49af8b498fc5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio of a Quadrantal Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "23de6cbe-0e4f-508e-a955-0d48dbdd93ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-3865" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\sec \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\n$$\n\nis a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to the point $(0,1)$.\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)} = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sec{\\theta} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sec{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)} = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos{\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)}} = \\dfrac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sec\\left(\\dfrac {\\pi} 2\\right)\n$$\n\nis undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054522 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b307f2-1d37-5741-8972-cbddae50c4c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Product of Two Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9a523530-ec38-5df8-997f-2f94975eb87b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3912" :question/problem "If $f(x) = 2x - 3$ and $g(x) = x + 6$, then $(fg)(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means the product of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\nTo find the product of the two functions $f$ and $g$, we multiply them together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (fg)(x) &= f(x)\\cdot g(x) \\\\[3pt] &= (2x - 3)(x + 6) \\\\[3pt] &= 2x^2 + 12x -3x - 18 \\\\[3pt] &= 2x^2 + 9x - 18 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054612 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d291ea-d722-5852-9c3a-faaba8e6df3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Quotient of Two Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "79c8f0a9-6f05-515f-abaa-553863402a06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3913" :question/problem "Find $(f/g)(-1)$ for $f(x) = 2x +5$ and $g(x) = x^3+2$." :question/worked-solution "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-1)$, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1)$, and then divide the results:\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 2(-1) + 5 = 3 \\\\\ng(-1) &= (-1)^{3} + 2 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(-1) = \\dfrac{f(-1)}{g(-1)} = \\dfrac{3}{1} =3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(x) = \\frac{2x + 5}{x^{3} + 2}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f/g)(-1) &= (2(-1) + 5)/((-1)^{3} + 2) = \\frac{3}{1} = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
